A||N||21||RDosdorian||88 GT Suspension Question (V-8)||||1||
Z||000000||RDosdorian||06-23-2000||02:48 PM||||When you give the Fiero a significant amount of extra ponies, you create problems in other areas.  I am referring to the suspension of the 88 GT.  Under normal or just above normal (stock) conditions, this stock suspension system works well, but in my case I have problems.<P>I have a 1995 LT1 motor producing 300 horsepower.  When I step on the gas (for hard acceleration) the rear end squats (like it is supposed to) and it lifts the front end.  You don’t notice this as much with the 2.8.  <P>I posted this problem to another list and they said I should go to Ryane Motorsports and get some Coil overs.  I do not want to go this route because I just put new Koni’s in the car and you know how expensive they are.  So I have decided that the best thing to do might be to have some custom springs made.<P>So my main questions are…  What is the stock spring compression rate on an 88 GT?  How much should I add to take care of my problem?  Should I add a significant amount to the front springs as well?  Or am I just going to have these problems and there is nothing I can do about it?<P>The Fiero Store sells a set of springs that lower the car 1 inch and increase the compression rate 12%.  I don’t think that is enough for me in the rear.  That may be enough in the front though.<P>Anybody else experience these problems that I am trying my best to describe?  What did you do to rectify the problem?<P>Any help would be appreciated.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||06-23-2000||03:42 PM||||Although my car is an 86, I put on the Eibach springs from the Fiero Store. These plus the poly bushings and your present Koni's should be enough. As an example of how stiff the new springs really are, With stock springs I put 200lbs on the back and the car lowered .75 inches. With the new springs and bushings it now lowers .25 inches. A friend has an 88 Formula with a 250hp 350 V-8 and between the springs and the bushings he has no problems. (he's using KYB struts which are a little softer than Koni's)||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||06-23-2000||04:46 PM||||The reason everyone recommends rear coil-overs is because you can customize the spring rates so easily. With an engine making that much power, you are going to need 350# to 400# rear spring rate, depending on how much performance driving and/or racing you do. If this is mainly a street cruiser, you can probably get away with a set of Eibach lowering springs and then crank your Konis up to the stiffest setting. Try to lighten up some weight from the rear, if you haven't moved the battery to the front, I'd seriously consider that too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||06-23-2000||04:49 PM||||Oh yeah the friend with the 88 formula has his battery up front also. Thanks BatBoy for reminding me.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||RDosdorian||06-23-2000||06:12 PM||||The battery has already been moved to the front, and Eibach does not make springs for the 88 application.  At least we have not been able to find them.  If you have a source for them, I would be interested.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||06-23-2000||07:38 PM||||I just whipped out my latest JC Whitless and noticed they didn't have any S/T springs listed for the 88 either. The only place that I readily found rear lowering springs for 88 Fieros is the Fiero Store (1-800-343-7648). They have a website too, I think.<P>I just reread your first post and noticed you already know about the Fiero Store. I think those are Eibach springs. I've noticed that Eibach claims to lower 1" and S/T claims about 1-1/2", but in reality, after driving for a while and waiting until everything settles, you can add another 1/2" to the drop in both cases. Also, at that point, you can play around with tire size to finish getting that look you want. I think the Eibach springs and the Koni shocks/struts work just fine unless you plan on mainly racing SCCA courses or something. I bet that small block is awesome...you're cranking out a little more power than my tired V8, but it's still loads of fun.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 06-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Spyder||06-23-2000||08:16 PM||||Here is something I found out, you can’t convert the rear Konis to coil overs with the Ryane kit.<BR>I can’t seem to find the magical answer for the lifting of the front.(I've been pondering for a while) This is what I would do: the stiffer  lowering springs from the Fiero Store all the way around, poly bushings on the a arms and the sway bars and then go from there.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||06-23-2000||09:45 PM||||If he's talking about a lot of front end lift during a drag racing launch, there really isn't much you can do except stiffen up the suspension. Rear wheel drive cars with a lot of torque tend to squat in the rear and pull the front up. This is not totally a bad thing. It levers more weight to the rear which helps traction. If he means lifting the front at high speed, that's a different problem.||||reg||1
Z||000008||RDosdorian||06-23-2000||09:47 PM||||Batboy…Maybe I should have told the whole story.  I didn’t because I didn’t think anybody would get into tire sizes as they really do not have anything to go with my lifting problem.  At any rate, the tires that will be going on the car will be 215/45ZR17 in front and 235/45ZR17 in the rear.  Now, I have S/T springs on my 87 GT.  I like them, but this is a wasted point because they don’t make springs for the 88.  As for who makes the Fiero Store’s springs, I don’t have a clue.  But as I stated in an earlier post, I do not know if the 12% increase in spring compression is enough.  That is why I am considering custom springs.<P>Spyder…As I said, I don’t know if the Fiero Store’s springs are enough for my application.  I assume the fronts will work, but I am doubtful of the rears unless the 12% brings them up to around 350#.  That is why I would like to know what the stock rate is.  As for poly bushings on the A arms and Sway Bars, what will that do for me.  I know what the poly bushings are supposed to do, but I don’t quite see how they will help with the lifting problem.<P>It is my opinion that when the car is under power, the rear springs are allowing too much of a squat thus allowing the front end to lift.  If I can get more in the rear spring compression rate I think that will limit the amount of squat and take care of the problem.  I will still lower the car a little either way.<P>Since it is everyone’s opinion that we need to get more spring rate in the rear.  I think I will go to the 400# in the rear and I will go about 15% over whatever stock is in the front.  Hopefully that will take care of things.<P>I am planning on doing this at the beginning of next week, so if anybody else has any ideas, I am all ears.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||RDosdorian||06-23-2000||09:54 PM||||I am getting the lift during a drag racing type of launch.  I can accept that but what I don't like is the way it during regular shifting.  If the springs are bad and losing there ability to do their job correctly, then they need to be replaced.  It is my hope that with higher spring rates I can limit some of the lifting.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||06-23-2000||09:56 PM||||The Fiero Store Springs are Eibach's. When I first put mine on the strut caps barely compressed the springs. I called the FS and they gave me a 1-800 number for Eibach to help me with my concerns. You are correct the Eibachs are 350# rates. (stock GT's are suppose to be 275# but this isn't 100% confirmed)<BR>Poly bushings will remove the flex out of the control arms and dampen the rate they move. This plus the springs works well. <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 06-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Archie||06-23-2000||11:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RDosdorian:<BR><B>I am getting the lift during a drag racing type of launch.  I can accept that but what I don't like is the way it during regular shifting.  If the springs are bad and losing there ability to do their job correctly, then they need to be replaced.  It is my hope that with higher spring rates I can limit some of the lifting.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ray, as we've discussed, I've driven many V-8 powered 88 fieros & have never seen as much lift as we saw on your car. In the last couple years I've driven:<P>PaulV's First V-8 powered Formula, it had a TPI LM1. With Poly bushings being the only suspension mod.  <P>PaulV's Second V-8 powered Formula, it had a TPI LM1. With Poly bushings, Adjustable Konis and just about everything else Paul could find to spend $$$ on.<P>G. Smith's (of NIFE) 88 LM1 TPI with a totally stock (high mile) suspension.  When built about 2 years ago this car launched level and now (he visited my shop yesterday) with 25K miles on the V-8 swap and a set of Coil Overs on the rear it still launches level.<P>My '88 IMSA V-8 car with a ZZ-4 and TPI. Has poly bushings and springs cut to lower the car. Has about 10K miles on new Monroe shock/struts. This car launches straight & level even when the tires are spinning.<P>Jack Wagner (of NIFE) 88 V-8 TargetMaster engine. This car has changed owners about as often as I change underwear. This car was originally a Zumwalt until Jack blew the clutch sky high while losing a drag race against me & my Blue Chop Top car. Now it's an Archie. With several owners, I don't know exactly what all has been done to the suspension. This car also launches very level, it's just not as fast as my old Blue Chop top.<P>With all these 88's & more and with all the '84 thru '87 cars I've done I've never seen one that has as much of a "Nose Up" launch as your car. I think that there is no doubt that you can get the front tires off the ground anytime you'd like. Your front lift is at least 2 times maybe 3 times as much as I've ever seen. I think with all the front lift you have that the rear "squat" is magnified.<P>As I've enumerated above, none of the above cars has had custom springs made. Based on everything I've seen, I'd suggest that you're stock springs have lost their "spring", I'd consider putting a good or new set of original springs on it (with shocks & poly bushings) and see what it does.<P>BTW, you made a comment about Poly bushings and how they wouldn't do anything to correct the condition.  I disagree, For example.....let's look at the front suspension. Under acceleration the "A" arms move down on their pivot points. When the "A" arms have worn rubber bushings they can also move inboard on acceleration thus allowing more "nose lift".<P>Hope this helps<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000012||84 Indy||06-23-2000||11:29 PM||||I would really check out and figure out what you need. I also have a 69 chevelle that has been set up for drag racing. It runs low 8's. One of the things i found out was if the springs are too stiff all you do is spin. I found this out the expensive way i have 4 sets of springs. Call me thick headed because i didn't listen to what people were saying. I knew better than they did. YEH RIGHT||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||06-24-2000||12:02 AM||||Once having a 68 Chevelle SS 396, I can safely say there is little comparison between them and Fieros. I agree with using the poly bushings for more control, if the original bushing are worn. I really think you'll be happy with front and rear Eibach springs, use your Konis, and seriously consider the poly bushings. It's got to be much better than what you have now.||||reg||1
Z||000014||RDosdorian||06-24-2000||01:31 AM||||While I have not changed the bushings on the sway bars.  The bushings on the A arms are brand new.  Changed in the last 150 miles.  We replaced them with stock GM bushings.  It appears that you guys would rather have poly, but for now they are rubber.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Dan86gt3.8||06-25-2000||03:11 AM||||Are you saying you are lifting the front tires on street tires?  If so thats awesome!   Also am I correct in assuming that archie built your car or you used one fo his kits?||||reg||1
Z||000016||RDosdorian||06-25-2000||04:10 AM||||Dan,<P>Archie did build my car and yes we believe that we can infact get the front tires off the ground.  Yes it sounds awesome and in the right conditions it might even be awesome, but for a street car/scca race car, this is not the desired way I want the car to react.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Oreif||06-25-2000||06:24 AM||||RDosdorian, You stated before you have the KONI struts on the back. Are these the adjustable ones? I'm not familar with the KONI's but I thought they had a soft/firm/firmer adjustment on them. Do you happen to know what they are set at if it's the adjustable ones? Maybe it could just be a simple adjustment of the strut. Just throwing Idea's out of area's to check.||||reg||1
Z||000018||lowCG||06-25-2000||10:49 AM||||If the newer Konis are anything like the old ones,the adjustment will only affect the rebound control,and it sounds like you need alot more of that in the front.<BR>Adding more rebound control in the front would help to solve the pitching problem without having to stiffen up the rear excessively,which will make the car like a shopping cart over the bumps.<BR>When my four cylinder was lifting it's nose,the problem was sloppy front-end parts,springs/shocks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||DiggityDan||06-25-2000||10:58 AM||||wheelie-bars??????||||reg||1
Z||000020||RDosdorian||06-25-2000||10:00 PM||||Wheelie Bars sound good.<P>As for the adjustments on the rear Koni'w, they are set at the firment setting.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Dan86gt3.8||06-26-2000||01:35 AM||||I guess I looked over scaa, I was thinking more along the lines of striaght line.  Like if I rooled up in something like that on the local strip here and got on it at a stop light and yanked the fronts, the guys would crap there pants.  And I assume you are doing this on street tires, so after they saw you pull the fronts and they are asking what kind of slicks you have and you can go ummmmmm..... they are some peb boys futura's and then could really throw them off.  Well I am rambling right now, but would like to do that someday!||||reg||1
